### Upcasting (always safe)

Upcasting assigns a subclass object to a parent reference. It is implicit and always safe because a subclass IS-A parent type. After upcasting, you can only call methods defined on the reference type, but overridden methods still run the subclass version – this is polymorphism.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class Account {
    protected BigDecimal balance;

    public Account(BigDecimal balance) {
        this.balance = balance;
    }

    public BigDecimal getBalance() { return balance; }

    public BigDecimal calculateInterest() {
        return BigDecimal.ZERO;  // base: no interest
    }
}

public class SavingsAccount extends Account {
    private double interestRate;

    public SavingsAccount(BigDecimal balance, double rate) {
        super(balance);
        this.interestRate = rate;
    }

    @Override
    public BigDecimal calculateInterest() {
        return balance.multiply(BigDecimal.valueOf(interestRate / 100));
    }

    public double getInterestRate() { return interestRate; }
}
```

```java
SavingsAccount sa = new SavingsAccount(new BigDecimal("500000"), 4.0);
Account a = sa;  // upcasting: implicit, safe

a.getBalance();           // OK: Account method
// a.getInterestRate();   // COMPILE ERROR: Account does not have this method
a.calculateInterest();    // runs SavingsAccount.calculateInterest() → 20000
```

The reference type `Account` limits what you can call. The object type `SavingsAccount` determines which implementation runs.

### Downcasting (needs check)

Downcasting casts a parent reference back to a subclass type. It requires an explicit cast and can throw ClassCastException if the actual object is not the expected type.

```java
Account a = new SavingsAccount(new BigDecimal("500000"), 4.0);

// Safe downcast with pattern matching (Java 16+)
if (a instanceof SavingsAccount sa) {
    System.out.println("Rate: " + sa.getInterestRate());  // SavingsAccount-specific method
}

// Traditional safe downcast
if (a instanceof SavingsAccount) {
    SavingsAccount sa2 = (SavingsAccount) a;
    System.out.println("Rate: " + sa2.getInterestRate());
}

// Unsafe downcast: ClassCastException at runtime
Account b = new CurrentAccount(new BigDecimal("250000"));
// SavingsAccount wrong = (SavingsAccount) b;  // ClassCastException!
```

### Reference type vs object type

This is the core concept behind polymorphism and one of the most important things to understand for interviews.

```java
Account a = new SavingsAccount(new BigDecimal("500000"), 4.0);
// Reference type: Account     → decides WHAT you can call (compile time)
// Object type: SavingsAccount → decides WHICH version runs (runtime)

a.getBalance();           // Account has this → allowed at compile time
// a.getInterestRate();   // Account does not have this → COMPILE ERROR
a.calculateInterest();    // SavingsAccount version runs (polymorphism)
```

### How is this used in banking service design?

Upcasting enables polymorphic processing of different account types through a common interface.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

public class InterestService {
    // Process all account types uniformly through the parent reference
    public BigDecimal calculateTotalInterest(List<Account> accounts) {
        BigDecimal total = BigDecimal.ZERO;
        for (Account account : accounts) {
            // Each account type's calculateInterest() runs automatically
            total = total.add(account.calculateInterest());
        }
        return total;
    }
}

// Usage:
List<Account> accounts = List.of(
    new SavingsAccount(new BigDecimal("500000"), 4.0),
    new CurrentAccount(new BigDecimal("250000")),
    new SavingsAccount(new BigDecimal("100000"), 3.5)
);
interestService.calculateTotalInterest(accounts);  // polymorphic dispatch
```

The service does not need to know which specific account type it is processing. Each type's overridden `calculateInterest()` runs automatically.

### What are common mistakes?

- **Downcasting without instanceof.** Throws ClassCastException at runtime. Always check first.
- **Confusing compile-time type with runtime type.** The reference type limits what you can call. The object type decides what runs.
- **Using instanceof chains instead of polymorphism.** If you find yourself writing `if (a instanceof SavingsAccount) ... else if (a instanceof CurrentAccount)`, consider adding the method to Account and overriding in each subclass.

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Pattern matching (Java 16+):** `if (a instanceof SavingsAccount sa)` combines the check and cast in one clean statement, eliminating the risk of a cast error.
- **Sealed classes (Java 17+)** with switch enable exhaustive type matching without instanceof chains: `switch (account) { case SavingsAccount sa -> ... case CurrentAccount ca -> ... }`.
- **Visitor pattern** is the design-pattern alternative to instanceof when you need different behavior per subtype without modifying the type hierarchy.
- At National Bank of India, Rajesh designs account processing around polymorphism so that adding a new account type (like FixedDepositAccount) requires only a new subclass with an overridden method, not changes to every service that processes accounts.
